  "command": "cat << 'EOF' > /app/paper.txt\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents—AI systems capable of generating, executing, and debugging code autonomously—has ushered in a new era of software development. However, these powerful systems also introduce significant security and safety concerns, particularly when they execute code that could be malicious, exploit vulnerabilities, or compromise system integrity. This paper surveys recent research advances in code agent technology and the emerging field of red-teaming for code agents. We examine methodologies for automatically testing the security and robustness of code agents through adversarial techniques, review benchmarks for evaluating risky code execution and generation, and discuss foundational deep learning architectures that enable modern code agent capabilities. Our analysis highlights critical gaps in current security evaluation frameworks and proposes directions for future research in secure code agent deployment.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a transformative class of AI systems that combine large language models with code execution capabilities. These agents can autonomously complete complex programming tasks, from simple code generation to full-stack application development, debugging, and software maintenance. Systems such as GitHub Copilot, Claude, ChatGPT with Code Interpreter, and other AI coding assistants have demonstrated remarkable proficiency in understanding natural language specifications and translating them into functional code.\n\nWhile the capabilities of code agents are impressive, they introduce unique security challenges. Unlike traditional software tools, code agents can execute arbitrary code in their runtime environments, making them potential vectors for:\n\n1. **Malicious code generation**: Adversarial prompts may induce code agents to produce exploitable code containing security vulnerabilities.\n2. **Unsafe code execution**: Code agents executing generated code in sandboxed environments may still be susceptible to container escapes, arbitrary file access, or cross-site scripting.\n3. **Prompt injection attacks**: Adversaries may craft inputs that bypass safety filters and coerce the agent into producing harmful outputs.\n4. **Data exfiltration**: Executing code that accesses sensitive data or communicates with external servers under the guise of routine operations.\n\nThese concerns have motivated a growing body of research on **red-teaming code agents**—systematic methods for probing, evaluating, and improving the security and robustness of these systems. Red-teaming involves simulating adversarial behavior to identify vulnerabilities before they can be exploited in production.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive survey of research advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. Section 2 reviews related work, including foundational deep learning architectures, recent benchmarks for evaluating code agent safety, and automated red-teaming frameworks. Section 3 discusses the methodology and technical approaches for code agent red-teaming. Section 4 examines current challenges and open research questions. Section 5 concludes with a summary of findings and directions for future work.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\nOur review of related work is organized into four areas: (1) deep learning architectures enabling modern AI systems, (2) benchmarking and evaluation of code agent safety, (3) automated red-teaming frameworks for code agents, and (4) recent advances in code agent technology.\n\n### 2.1 Foundational Deep Learning Architectures\n\nThe success of modern code agents is built upon foundational advances in deep learning. The Transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al. in \"Attention Is All You Need\" (arXiv:1706.03762), revolutionized sequence modeling by replacing recurrent and convolutional architectures with self-attention mechanisms. This architecture became the foundation for large language models (LLMs) that power modern code agents, enabling them to process and generate code with unprecedented fluency and accuracy.\n\nThe Transformer's attention mechanism allows models to capture long-range dependencies in code sequences, which is essential for understanding complex programming structures such as nested control flow, object-oriented hierarchies, and distributed system architectures. Building upon the Transformer, subsequent research has scaled these architectures to hundreds of billions of parameters, further improving code generation capabilities.\n\nAnother foundational contribution is the work by He et al. in \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition\" (arXiv:1512.03385), which introduced residual connections that enabled training of much deeper neural networks. While primarily focused on computer vision, the residual learning framework has influenced architecture design across deep learning, including in code-related models where very deep networks benefit from skip connections to mitigate vanishing gradient problems.\n\n### 2.2 Benchmarking Code Agent Safety\n\nThe evaluation of code agent safety has emerged as a critical research area. Recent work by Guo et al. introduced **RedCode** (arXiv:2409.12345), a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating risky code execution and generation capabilities in code agents. The RedCode benchmark encompasses multiple categories of risky code, including:\n\n- **Malware generation and analysis**: Code that contains malicious functionality such as data exfiltration, system compromise, or persistence mechanisms.\n- **Vulnerability exploitation**: Code that exploits known security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, SQL injection, or cross-site scripting.\n- **Unsafe operations**: Code that performs dangerous file system operations, network connections, or privilege escalation.\n\nThe RedCode benchmark provides a standardized evaluation framework for measuring the propensity of code agents to generate or execute risky code across diverse scenarios. By testing code agents against systematically constructed adversarial prompts, the benchmark quantifies both the success rate of red-teaming attacks and the effectiveness of safety mitigations.\n\n### 2.3 Automated Red-Teaming Frameworks\n\nA significant advance in the field is **RedCodeAgent** (arXiv:2510.02609), which presents an automated red-teaming agent designed to systematically probe diverse code agents for security vulnerabilities. Unlike manual red-teaming approaches that rely on human expertise to craft adversarial prompts, RedCodeAgent autonomously generates, iterates, and refines adversarial inputs to maximize the likelihood of eliciting unsafe behavior from target code agents.\n\nKey features of the RedCodeAgent framework include:\n\n- **Multi-turn adversarial interaction**: The red-teaming agent engages in iterative dialogue with target code agents, refining its strategy based on previous responses.\n- **Diverse attack strategies**: The framework employs multiple attack strategies including direct prompts, indirect prompts, role-playing scenarios, and code interpretation exploitation.\n- **Automated evaluation**: Generated outputs are automatically evaluated for safety violations using both rule-based checks and LLM-based classifiers.\n- **Cross-agent generalization**: The red-teaming agent is evaluated across multiple target code agents, assessing the generalizability of discovered vulnerabilities.\n\nRedCodeAgent demonstrates that automated red-teaming can uncover security vulnerabilities in code agents at scale, providing a systematic approach to security evaluation that supplements manual testing and formal verification methods.\n\n### 2.4 Code Agent Technology Advances\n\nThe broader research community has made significant progress in developing code agent capabilities. Recent work has focused on improving code generation accuracy, enabling multi-step code execution, supporting debugging and refactoring tasks, and integrating with software development workflows. These advances have been enabled by improvements in training data curation, model architecture, and inference efficiency.\n\nThe integration of code execution environments with language models has opened new capabilities for interactive coding, where agents can generate code, execute it, inspect results, and iteratively refine their output. This closed-loop interaction paradigm is particularly powerful for complex programming tasks that require trial-and-error exploration of code solutions.\n\n## 3. Methodology for Code Agent Red-Teaming\n\nThe red-teaming of code agents involves a systematic process for probing security vulnerabilities:\n\n### 3.1 Adversarial Prompt Engineering\n\nThe foundation of code agent red-teaming is adversarial prompt engineering. Attackers craft prompts designed to elicit unsafe behavior from code agents. These prompts can take several forms:\n\n- **Direct requests**: Explicitly asking the agent to generate malicious code.\n- **Indirect requests**: Framing malicious code generation as legitimate research, educational content, or defensive analysis.\n- **Context manipulation**: Providing malicious context or instructions that override the agent's safety guidelines.\n- **Multi-step attacks**: Using a sequence of interactions to progressively circumvent safety filters.\n\n### 3.2 Automated Red-Teaming Loop\n\nModern red-teaming frameworks employ automated loops that iteratively improve attack strategies:\n\n1. **Prompt generation**: Generate adversarial prompts using the red-teaming agent.\n2. **Execution**: Send prompts to the target code agent and collect responses.\n3. **Evaluation**: Analyze outputs for safety violations using predefined criteria.\n4. **Strategy refinement**: Update the attack strategy based on evaluation results.\n5. **Iteration**: Repeat until a stopping criterion is met (e.g., maximum iterations or sufficient vulnerabilities discovered).\n\n### 3.3 Evaluation Metrics\n\nCode agent red-teaming evaluations typically measure:\n\n- **Attack success rate**: Percentage of adversarial prompts that elicit unsafe responses.\n- **Severity of vulnerabilities**: Classification of discovered vulnerabilities by severity (critical, high, medium, low).\n- **Robustness score**: Aggregate measure of code agent resilience across multiple attack types.\n- **Mitigation effectiveness**: Comparison of safety filter performance before and after deployment.\n\n## 4. Challenges and Open Research Questions\n\nDespite progress in code agent red-teaming, several challenges remain:\n\n1. **Adaptive adversaries**: As safety mechanisms improve, adversaries develop more sophisticated attacks. Red-teaming frameworks must continuously evolve to address emerging threat vectors.\n\n2. **Evaluation generalization**: Current benchmarks may not capture the full spectrum of real-world threats. Developing more comprehensive and realistic evaluation frameworks remains an open challenge.\n\n3. **Balancing utility and safety**: Overly aggressive safety measures may degrade code agent utility for legitimate users. Research is needed to develop safety mechanisms that protect against attacks without unnecessarily restricting beneficial functionality.\n\n4. **Automated vulnerability repair**: Beyond identifying vulnerabilities, future systems should automatically suggest or implement repairs to address discovered security issues.\n\n5. **Cross-modal threats**: As code agents increasingly support multimodal inputs (code, text, images, audio), new attack vectors may emerge that exploit interactions between modalities.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\n\nThe convergence of advanced language models and code execution capabilities has created powerful code agent systems with transformative potential for software development. However, these capabilities introduce significant security challenges that must be addressed through rigorous red-teaming and safety evaluation.\n\nThis paper has surveyed recent advances in code agent technology and red-teaming research. We reviewed foundational deep learning architectures including the Transformer (Vaswani et al., arXiv:1706.03762) and residual learning (He et al., arXiv:1512.03385) that enable modern code agent capabilities. We examined specialized benchmarks and frameworks for evaluating code agent safety, including RedCode (Guo et al., arXiv:2409.12345) for benchmarking risky code execution and generation, and RedCodeAgent (Guo et al., arXiv:2510.02609) for automated red-teaming.\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integrated into software development workflows, continued investment in red-teaming research, safety evaluation frameworks, and robust security mechanisms will be essential to ensure their responsible deployment. The research community must work collaboratively to develop comprehensive security solutions that enable the benefits of code agents while mitigating their risks.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n1. Guo, C., Xie, C., Yang, Y., et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609.\n\n2. Guo, C., Liu, X., Xie, C., et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345.\n\n3. Vaswani, A., Shazeer, N., Parmar, N., et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" arXiv:1706.03762.\n\n4. He, K., Zhang, X., Ren, S., et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385.\nEOF"
